# KNOWN VACUOUS -- DO NOT ENABLE IN A GATING RUN AS WRITTEN.
#
# THIS FILE IS TRACKED AND NO GATE READS IT, ON PURPOSE. It lives under
# test/draft/, whose one property is that every recursive .ci reader skips it
# (test/draft/README.md names the four readers and the ratchet that holds them
# there), and it is tracked by an explicit .gitignore negation rather than by
# accident. It is NOT an orphan a suite forgot to name: moving it to
# test/l2tp/ would gate a file that passes against the BROKEN build, which is
# worse than not running it at all.
#
# What would have to change for it to become gated: a failure-injection point
# that does not route through ptrace, so that the pre-fix build fails the
# assertions below and the fixed build passes them. The candidate is named at
# the end of this header. With that in hand, move this file to test/l2tp/,
# delete the .gitignore exception, and it is gated like any other test.
#
# It is kept rather than deleted because the mechanism, the measured numbers
# and the reason they do not discriminate are what the next attempt needs, and
# a spec section cannot carry a runnable scenario
# (spec-subscriber-reader-loops-retry-a-failing-socket-without-backoff,
# Phase 6, AC-1's end-to-end proof; the owner ruled on 2026-09-11 that AC-1
# resolves on the unit coverage and that this file is committed here).
# The short version of the finding:
#
# Goal: put readLoop's UDP socket (listener.go) into a persistently failing
# state from OUTSIDE the daemon and show the pacer keeps CPU low, logs, and
# counts, where the pre-fix bare `continue` would spin a core.
#
# No ordinary external network condition can sustain a repeating read error
# on any of the four loops this spec covers: a UDP or AF_PACKET socket that
# is not tied to one interface via a specific bind (all four are) delivers
# SILENCE on interface-down (recvfrom blocks with no data), never an error,
# and even a socket that IS bound to one interface only gets ENETDOWN
# delivered ONCE from the kernel (net/packet/af_packet.c, packet_notifier)
# before it reverts to blocking -- never a sustained stream.
#
# The one mechanism that CAN sustain a repeating error is ptrace-based
# syscall fault injection: `strace -f --seccomp-bpf -e trace=recvfrom
# -e inject=recvfrom:error=ENETDOWN:when=1+ -- ze -` (wrapped in a tmpfs
# script because the .ci `cmd=` line's own `:<word>=` key parser collides
# with strace's `:error=`/`:when=` syntax otherwise). Run BY HAND against a
# throwaway daemon (not through this .ci file), it works exactly as
# intended: `ze_l2tp_listener_read_errors_total` reached 36640 and
# `process_cpu_seconds_total` reached 0.99 in under 4 seconds against the
# pre-fix (pacer-reverted) code -- a real, reproduced busy loop.
#
# Run through THIS .ci file, against the SAME pre-fix code, it is vacuous:
# the pacer-reverted build reports the SAME low CPU and a SIMILAR small
# counter delta (19 errors in one run) as the fixed build, so the two are
# indistinguishable and every expect= line below passes on BOTH. Root
# cause, confirmed by inspecting the captured strace log: Go's runtime
# sends itself a constant stream of SIGURG signals for goroutine
# preemption, and ptrace traps on EVERY signal delivery regardless of
# `--seccomp-bpf` (that flag only elides the trap for UNMATCHED syscalls,
# never for signals). Each trap is a stop-the-world round trip into the
# tracer, and time spent ptrace-stopped counts as wall clock, not as the
# tracee's own CPU time (getrusage). The tracer's own overhead therefore
# throttles the traced daemon's iteration rate to the same order of
# magnitude the pacer's 250ms ceiling already caps it to (in the captured
# run: 19 iterations in the observation window, versus the pacer's own
# steady-state ceiling of about 12), so BOTH the CPU signal and the
# iteration-rate signal this test needs to read are swamped by the
# measuring instrument itself. This is not a tunable threshold problem:
# no CPU or counter bound distinguishes the two builds under this
# mechanism, because ptrace's overhead dominates over the range where they
# would otherwise differ.
#
# What would actually work: a fault-injection point that does not route
# through ptrace, e.g. a kernel fault-injection framework
# (docs.kernel.org/fault-injection) targeting the socket receive path, or
# an LD_PRELOAD shim -- ruled out here because Ze's binaries are
# CGO_ENABLED=0 and make raw syscalls directly, so libc interposition
# reaches nothing. Neither was pursued further in this phase.
option=needs-linux

tmpfs=run-strace.sh:mode=755:terminator=EOF_SCRIPT
#!/bin/sh
exec strace -f --seccomp-bpf -e trace=recvfrom -e inject=recvfrom:error=ENETDOWN:when=1+ -o strace-listener.log -- ze -
EOF_SCRIPT

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
l2tp {
	enabled true
	shared-secret s3cr3t
}
environment {
	l2tp {
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT
		}
	}
}

telemetry {
	prometheus {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
option=env:var=ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe:value=true

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=./run-strace.sh:stdin=config
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture l2tp/failing-socket $PORT2:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: CPU use stayed at
expect=stdout:contains=OK: ze_l2tp_listener_read_errors_total rose by
expect=stderr:contains=l2tp: listener read error
